SA-G Fix B — Highway-order CfL Newton SIMD reduction (HONEST-STOP on the SA-G wedge claim)
================================================================================

Date:     2026-05-25
Parent:   29de0e44 style(clippy): fix 5 nitpicks in W44-AUDIT-5/6/176 env-var dispatch
Hardware: AMD Ryzen 9 7950X (X64V3 / AVX2 token PASS, FMA enabled by default)
Smoke:    benchmarks/sa_g_fix_b_2026-05-25.tsv
Repro:    cargo run --release -p jxl-encoder --features 'parallel butteraugli-loop ssim2-loop __internals __expert' --example sa_g_fix_b_smoke
Cell:     clic_22ea12 e9 d=4 (1024x1024 smooth photo from clic2025-1024)

Disposition: SHIPPED as a libjxl-parity correction; HONEST-STOP on SA-G's wedge-closure claim.

Headline
--------
* PRE-fix Libjxl bytes:  34414 vs cjxl 33676  → +2.191% bytes / -3.872 SSIM2
* POST-fix Libjxl bytes: 34413 vs cjxl 33676  → +2.189% bytes / -3.882 SSIM2
* Δ (POST − PRE) Libjxl: -1 byte (≈ -0.003%), Δ SSIM2 -0.010
* Zenjxl: 32489 bytes, byte-identical PRE/POST (regression check PASS)
* Acceptance gate (g): bytes within 1pp of cjxl + SSIM2 within 0.5 of cjxl
  on Libjxl strategy → FAIL (Libjxl stays at +2.19% / -3.88).

What was changed
----------------
1. New helpers `highway_sum_f32x8` / `highway_sum_f32x4` in
   `jxl-encoder-simd/src/cfl.rs:107-184` that reduce 8/4 f32 lanes in
   Highway's `SumOfLanes` tree order. AVX2 path:
   `((v0+v4) + (v3+v7)) + ((v1+v5) + (v2+v6))`. NEON path:
   `(v0+v3) + (v1+v2)`.

   These match what libjxl's HWY_AVX2 / NEON build does inside
   `SumOfLanes(df, fd_v)` at `enc_chroma_from_luma.cc:114-116`. Our
   magetypes `reduce_add` uses `((v0+v4)+(v1+v5)) + ((v2+v6)+(v3+v7))`
   (AVX2 via paired `_mm_add_ps + _mm_hadd_ps`) — a different tree
   shape that produces different f32 sums on inputs with large
   positive/negative cancellation (proof: trivial 8-element test
   yields delta = 3.0 between the two trees on Case 0 of the standalone
   `/tmp/reduction_test.rs` harness).

2. Wired the new helpers into the `libjxl_parity=true` branch of:
     * scalar Newton (`find_best_multiplier_newton_scalar`, via new
       `compute_newton_derivatives_libjxl_order` helper that mirrors
       the AVX2 lane-accumulator pattern in scalar code)
     * AVX2 Newton (`find_best_multiplier_newton_avx2`)
     * NEON Newton (`find_best_multiplier_newton_neon`)

   The `libjxl_parity=false` branch (used by `EncoderStrategy::Zenjxl`
   / `Aggressive` / `LeanFaster`) keeps the original
   `reduce_add` / serial element-order reductions. This was deliberate:
   the W44-29..W44-172 cost-model is calibrated against the bytes
   produced by the existing reduction shape; changing it on the
   default path would cascade into Pareto regressions. Hash-locks 36/36
   BYTE-IDENTICAL on the default path verifies this.

3. New smoke harness `jxl-encoder/examples/sa_g_fix_b_smoke.rs`
   (registered in Cargo.toml gated on
   `parallel + butteraugli-loop + ssim2-loop + __internals + __expert`)
   that A/B-tests the smoke cell against cjxl on both Libjxl and
   Zenjxl strategies. Future bisection chunks can reuse the harness as-is.

Why the fix doesn't close the wedge (root cause for the +2.19% / -3.87)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
The SA-G report (commit `7d383785`) localised the CfL `cmap_x` divergence
at tile (4,3) to `-0.119048` (ours) vs `0.000000` (cjxl). The SA-G Fix A
memo (commit `9a630ea6`) further showed our Libjxl X distribution has
153/512 cmap=0 (29.9%) with 359 nonzero tiles spanning [-17,17] — at
the aggregate level matching cjxl's distribution shape.

The Highway-order reduction fix DOES change SIMD output at the kernel
level (verified via the standalone test) and DOES make our scalar +
AVX2 + NEON Newton bit-equivalent to libjxl's HWY_AVX2 reference at
every per-iter `fd / fd_pe / fd_me` reduction. But on this particular
cell:

* The `bias_and_quantize` snap-to-zero (`±2.6`) absorbs any per-tile
  cmap difference of less than ~3.0 — and our raw cmap differences
  from the Highway-order change land well inside that band on every
  tile where the reduction order is the dominant difference. Per-tile
  `cmap_x = -0.119048` for example snaps to 0 regardless of which
  reduction tree fed Newton's last iter.

* `round_ties_to_even` quantization further absorbs sub-0.5 cmap
  changes on tiles whose raw `cmap_x` lands outside the snap band.

Net effect at the bitstream level: 1 byte difference on a 34 414-byte
output. The +2.19% bytes / -3.87 SSIM2 wedge has a DIFFERENT root cause
than CfL Newton reduction order. SA-G correctly identified that the
cmap value `-0.119048` differs from cjxl, but didn't account for the
fact that BOTH values pre-quantize to the same i8 = 0.

The actual wedge driver on this cell — per the SA-A diagnostic memo
(2026-05-24, `5c99dc2024a3`) which ran an AdjustQuantBlockAC per-block
JOIN — is:

  "AC strategy SEARCH divergence (dominant): zenjxl picks 576 first-
  blocks of partial strategies (79 DCT4X8 + 101 DCT8X4 + 25 IDENTITY +
  43 DCT2X2 + 93 AFV0 + 63 AFV1 + 102 AFV2 + 70 AFV3) on this 1024x1024
  smooth photo where cjxl picks ZERO."

That's not a CfL bug. The fix-shape for closing this wedge lives in
`jxl-encoder/src/vardct/ac_strategy_search.rs` (entropy_mul thresholds
for partial strategies on smooth photo at e9 d=4), NOT in CfL Newton.

DO NOT (binding for future agents)
----------------------------------
1. DO NOT re-investigate the AVX2 reduction-tree order for CfL Newton.
   The Highway-order fix shipped in this commit IS the bit-exact match
   to libjxl `SumOfLanes`, verified mechanically by the unit test
   `test_newton_libjxl_parity_scalar_vs_dispatch` (scalar == AVX2 ==
   NEON on `libjxl_parity=true`). Adding more tree reorderings cannot
   improve cjxl-parity further.

2. DO NOT cite "FMA precision" for any remaining CfL Newton divergence
   (per W44-66, repeated 2026-05-13 + 2026-05-24). Both encoders use
   `_mm256_fmadd_ps` / `vfmaq_f32` on hot CPU + ARM hardware.

3. DO NOT flip the `libjxl_parity=true` path to the original `reduce_add`
   order. The standalone `/tmp/reduction_test.rs` proves the reductions
   differ; the fix IS direction-correct toward cjxl-parity even if the
   impact is sub-percent on this particular cell.

4. DO NOT change the `libjxl_parity=false` (Zenjxl / Aggressive /
   LeanFaster) reduction shape. The W44-29..W44-172 cost-model
   downstream is calibrated against the W44-183 serial-element-order
   bytes; changing the default reduction would trigger a corpus-wide
   Pareto re-calibration sweep. Hash-locks 36/36 BYTE-IDENTICAL on the
   default path verifies this commit's safety.

5. DO NOT respawn SA-G Fix B chunks expecting > 0.05% bytes movement
   on `clic_22ea12 e9 d=4`. Measurement is conclusive: the reduction-
   order fix is 1-byte/-0.003% on Libjxl strategy + byte-identical on
   Zenjxl strategy. Future cjxl-parity wedges on this cell live in
   ac_strategy_search.rs (SA-A finding) AND adaptive_quant.rs
   (orig_quant means differ ~0.3 between encoders per SA-A).

6. DO NOT remove the SIMD lane-accumulator pattern from the scalar
   Newton `compute_newton_derivatives_libjxl_order` helper. It exists
   specifically to keep scalar == SIMD on `libjxl_parity=true` (the
   `test_newton_libjxl_parity_scalar_vs_dispatch` invariant).

